      "status": "affected",
      "action_statement": "Update gradle-9 to 9.9.1-r0 or later.",
      "status_notes": "GHSA-gqrr-4j89-5267: the Gradle client trusts the daemon registry file, whose location a project can set through org.gradle.daemon.registry.base in its gradle.properties. It deserializes parts of the file, connects to any address listed there and sends it the build's environment, and a crafted reply can make a Tooling API client run attacker code. Gradle 9.0.0 to 9.9.0 is affected; 9.9.1 fixes it (https://github.com/gradle/gradle/security/advisories/GHSA-gqrr-4j89-5267). We read every gradle-9 package published for Alpine 3.23 in the DHI Alpine repository as of 2026-11-27: 22 versions, 9.4.0-r0 to 9.9.2-r0. Each aarch64 package has the same index checksum as the x86_64 package of that version (9.6.1-r3 and 9.6.1-r4 are published for x86_64 only), so we read the x86_64 packages, checked each against its index checksum, and read every jar in them, including nested jars. Each build ships the Gradle release of its package version: the gradle-core, gradle-launcher and gradle-test-kit jars are named for it, 9.4.0 in 9.4.0-r0 and 9.4.0-r1 and so on up to 9.9.0 in 9.9.0-r0, 9.9.1 in 9.9.1-r0 and 9.9.2 in 9.9.2-r0, and their manifests give the same version, with a -SNAPSHOT suffix from 9.8.0-r1 on. The fix adds the error text Unexpected address type, which is in org/gradle/launcher/daemon/registry/DaemonRegistryContent$Serializer in the gradle-daemon-protocol jar of 9.9.1-r0 and 9.9.2-r0 and in no class of 9.9.0-r0. Every build before 9.9.1-r0 ships Gradle 9.9.0 or earlier, so the range ends at 9.9.1-r0, the first build with Gradle 9.9.1."
    },
